    "content": "あなたは英語学習コンテンツの独立評価者です。以下の英語記事から「英語上級者が学ぶ価値のあるKey Phrase」を選ぶ課題があり、\n複数の匿名の選定結果(セット S1〜Sn、各5件以内)があります。各候補を下記rubricで採点してください。どのセットがどのモデルの結果かは不明です。\n\n【選定時に参加者へ与えられた条件(参考)】\n1. 本文中の連続した語句だけを抜き出す(一般化以外の変更・言い換え・追加・創作は禁止)。\n2. 一般化後5語以内。\n3. 固有名詞や特定の人物・物事を指す部分は、意味と文法を保って ~ / A / B / someone / something 等に置き換える(一般化できる場合は必ず行う)。\n4. 上級者でも学ぶ価値のある語彙・イディオム・慣用表現を優先する。\n5. 基本語だけで構成され字面どおりに意味が分かる表現や、上級者なら知っている易しい表現は除外する。\n6. 条件を満たす候補から5件選ぶ。適切な候補が不足する場合のみ5件未満とし、数合わせはしない。\n\n【採点rubric(各候補、各0〜2の整数)】\n- G 一般化の適切さ: 2=本文固有の固有名詞・特定の人物/物事が適切に一般化されている、または一般化すべき要素がなく原文のままで適切 / 1=一般化が不十分または過剰、あるいは軽微な不自然さ / 0=一般化すべき固有要素が残って学習価値を損なう、または一般化で意味・文法が壊れている。\n- V 上級者向け学習価値: 2=上級者でも学ぶ価値が明確(句動詞・イディオム・コロケーション・語法・語彙として) / 1=価値はあるが中程度、または一部平易 / 0=学習価値がほぼない。\n- E 易しさの排除: 2=易しい表現ではない(意味が字面から自明でない) / 1=やや易しいが慣用的な意味や語法の学びはある / 0=基本語のみで字面どおり、上級者なら当然知っている。\n- N 単位の自然さ: 2=自然で完結した学習単位 / 1=余分な語または不足があるが許容 / 0=中途半端に切られている・不自然。\n- needs_generalization: その候補の元の表現に、一般化(~, A/B, someone, something等)すべき要素が本文上あったか(true/false)。\nセット単位: padding = 5件を埋めるために学習価値の低い候補(V<=0またはE<=0)を入れていると判断するか(true/false)。\n\n【注意】\n- 本文との一致・語数は別途機械検証するため、ここでは採点対象外。ただし本文に明らかに存在しない表現と気付いた場合のみ noteに \"not_in_text\" と書く。\n- 候補の優劣を、セットの並び順やセットIDから推測しない。各候補を独立に採点する。\n- 候補が0件のセットは candidates を空配列にする。\n\n【出力】JSONのみ(説明文・コードフェンス不要)。noteは通常は空文字、本文に存在しない表現と気付いた場合のみ \"not_in_text\"。形式:\n{\"sets\":[{\"set_id\":\"S1\",\"padding\":false,\"candidates\":[{\"idx\":1,\"G\":2,\"V\":2,\"E\":1,\"N\":2,\"needs_generalization\":false,\"note\":\"\"}]}]}\n\n\n【記事】\n\n# A Human Joined an AI Phone Call. The Problem Was the “Cast Change” Notice\n\nI was impressed: “AI can even make phone calls. The future is here!” But the person handling the request on the other end of the line was human. Wait—what a sudden change of genre, from science fiction to a workplace drama. Still, the heart of this story is not “So it was a human after all!” It is whether people were properly told about the change in who was taking part.\n\nMeta was testing some calls made through Muse in which trained contract workers made the calls and completed the conversations with the other person. They tested a setup where Muse handed the work over to a human agent. Not all calls were handled by humans. It’s too soon to put up a caption saying, “Actually, it was all done by people!”\n\nBut one important notice was missing from this test. The vice president of Meta’s Superintelligence Labs division admitted in an internal post that starting a test in which contract workers made calls without proper disclosure was a “mistake.” The vice president also said that Meta had rolled back its human concierge feature for now.\n\nLet’s check the breaking-news caption once more. What they pulled back was the human concierge feature. They did not stop all calls through Muse. Canceling an entire show and pausing one segment are two different things.\n\nSo, is it bad in itself to bring in a human helper? There may be times when handing things to a person helps move the conversation forward. But there is a big difference between telling someone, “A person will take over from here,” and quietly changing the cast backstage. In a movie, you might clap and say, “Ah, so that’s who it is!” when a mysterious person is revealed. But you may not want a surprise reveal in a conversation you are part of.\n\nSome people may choose their words or feel differently when they think they are talking to AI than when they know they are talking to a person. Even if the request is handled without a problem, that does not mean the disclosure issue can be brushed aside with “it all worked out in the end.” That was exactly the point Meta itself admitted it had got wrong.\n\nConvenience matters. But for some people, knowing who they are talking to matters just as much. If AI hands the baton to a person, that one line saying so is part of the service, too. The person taking over does not have to be a secret guest.\n\n## In one line\nMeta admitted it was a mistake to have contractors handle some Muse calls without telling the people they called.\n\n\n【匿名の選定結果】\n\nS1:\n  1. change of genre\n  2. handing things to someone\n  3. brushed aside\n  4. worked out in the end\n  5. hands the baton to someone\n\nS2:\n  1. human concierge feature\n  2. roll back\n  3. brush aside\n  4. take over\n  5. work out\n\nS3:\n  1. without proper disclosure\n  2. rolled back ~\n  3. move the conversation forward\n  4. quietly changing the cast backstage\n  5. hands the baton\n\nS4:\n  1. concierge\n  2. proper disclosure\n  3. rolled back\n  4. brushed aside\n  5. hands the baton to someone\n\nS5:\n  1. on the other end of the line\n  2. rolled back its ~ feature\n  3. brushed aside with ~\n  4. hands the baton to ~\n  5. take over from here\n\nS6:\n  1. hands the baton to someone\n  2. be brushed aside\n  3. choose their words\n  4. handed something over to someone\n  5. rolled back something\n\nS7:\n  1. brushed aside\n  2. worked out in the end\n  3. choose their words\n  4. hands the baton to\n  5. rolled back ~\n"
